    "math_academy_id": "q-216042",
    "knowledge_point_id": "14370371-529b-5f25-9963-62c0cd9a172e",
    "knowledge_point": "Solving a System Containing Fractions in One Equation Using Elimination",
    "problem": "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3p+\\frac{4}{5}q=-1 \\\\ 9p-4q=-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.",
    "worked_solution": "To get rid of the fractions in the first equation, we must multiply the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $5.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3p+\\frac{4}{5}q=-1 & |\\,\\times 5 \\\\ 9p-4q=-13\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-15p+4q=-5 \\\\ 9p-4q=-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that the $4q$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-4q$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $q$-variable, and allows us to solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-15p+\\cancel{4q} & =-5 \\\\ 9p-\\cancel{4q} & =-13 \\\\ -6p & =-18 \\\\ p & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $p=3$ into the equation $9p-4q=-13$ to solve for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9p-4q & =-13 \\\\ 9\\cdot 3-4q & =-13 \\\\ 27-4q & =-13 \\\\ -4q & =-40 \\\\ q & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=3,\\,q=10.$",
